  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A cold front will cross the region today, producing \nseveral rounds of showers and scattered thunderstorms with uneven \ncoverage.\n\nGOES satellite and radar imagery shows multiple lines of showers and \nthunderstorms across western and north-central NY this afternoon. A \nmid-level trough will move across the forecast area this afternoon. \nA cold front resides west of the forecast area, with a pre-frontal \ntrough across western NY. Daytime heating and dewpoints in the mid \nto upper 60s has led to 1500-2000 J/kg of surface based instability \nacross the region. An area of 30 knots of effective shear resides \nfrom the Genesee Valley to the eastern Lake Ontario region. A Severe \nThunderstorm Watch is in effect for northern Cayuga, Oswego, and \nLewis counties until 9PM. A bowing segment or isolated storm may \nproduce damaging wind gusts, large hail, and heavy rainfall. \n\nA broken line of thunderstorms resides along a lake breeze boundary \nfrom Niagara county to eastern Lake Ontario. This activity is \nexpected to remain intact as it moves into Oswego county through \npeak heating. The eastern extent of this line may strengthen into \nstrong storms as it taps into stronger mid-level lapse rates and \nshear. A southwest flow has kept areas downwind of Lake Erie rain-\nfree this afternoon, however can't rule out a few showers moving \nsouth across northern Erie to Genesee counties late this afternoon \nthrough early this evening as the boundary drops south. Weaker lapse \nrates and shear will maintain the chance for non-severe \nthunderstorms across the western Southern Tier. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms will diminish and end this evening with \na cold front and the loss of daytime heating. \n\nKey Message 2...A weak secondary cold front may produce a few \nscattered light showers overnight through Friday morning, then dry \nweather returns for the weekend.\n\nA closed low will move across Hudson Bay with an associated trough \nmoving across southern Ontario and Quebec tonight. A secondary cold \nfront will move across the forecast area through early Friday \nmorning. Weak cold air advection and low-level moisture will support \ncloud cover and light showers overnight through Friday morning. \n\nSurface high pressure will be overhead later Friday through \nSunday and support dry weather across the region.",
